  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Friday...\n\nFollowing the shortwave and associated cold front that moved\nthrough this morning, increasing southwest flow is expected into\nSunday. Although models keep with the trend of splitting the\nstrongest energy to the north and south, weak energy in the low\nlevels look to provide enough forcing to potentially produce a few\nstronger thunderstorms Sunday evening into the overnight hours.\nOne caveat to the potential for thunderstorms Sunday evening is\nmodel soundings seeming pretty consistent with a cap or inversion,\npreventing thunderstorm development. That being said, if the\ninstability present (500-1000 J/kg MUCAPE) is able to be tapped\ninto, the bulk shear present (35-45kts) would support a few strong\nstorms capable of producing heavy rain, small hail and gusty\nwinds. Currently, there is about a 30-50% chance for at least a\nquarter inch of precipitation for the higher terrain and the\nMT/Dakotas border. Any stronger thunderstorm will be capable of\nproducing locally higher amounts with atmospheric moisture at\n150-200% of normal. As trailing energy from this system moves \nover the region, small chances for showers will remain in the \nforecast for Monday. As a more westerly flow aloft moves in \nTuesday, conditions look to become dry with seasonable \ntemperatures in place. Towards the end of the week there is a lot\nof spread, but ensembles are hinting at a cooler trough moving \nthrough. Will be something to watch through the week. TS",
